Why a warehouse became an RPG
The warehouse started from the game’s own fiction. If a cosmic being is harvesting the light of stars, that light has to be stored somewhere. But “a place where numbers sit” is not gameplay. So I looked at what other idle games do to make a secondary screen worth visiting, and the answer, over and over, is some version of an inventory with characters who progress. Heroes, workers, gear. This is a genre convention as old and as load-bearing as the tech tree itself.
I did not invent a new system. I reached for the oldest and most understood progression vocabulary there is — the role-playing trinity of Strength, Dexterity, and Intelligence — and gave it to a crew of warehouse workers. A player who has touched any RPG in the last forty years reads STR / DEX / INT without a tutorial. There is no reason to design a novel stat system when a universally legible one is sitting right there. This is the same principle that runs through the whole project: use the proven wheel, spend your energy elsewhere.
Workers with silly names
The first decision was the one that mattered most, and it is almost nothing in code. The workers have names. Not “Worker #1” and “Worker #2” — actual names, and deliberately soft, silly ones. In the code comment I called them “puppies”:
"workerNames": [
"Fluffy", "Sunny", "Smarty", "Grumpy", "Lucky",
"Muffin", "Bagel", "Barky", "Zoomie", "Button",
"Cupcake", "Ginger", "Bean"
]A worker named Grumpy who is slowly leveling up is someone you feel something about. “Unit 07” is not. That is the entire reason the names exist — attachment is cheap to create and it changes how the screen feels to sit with. The names live in the localization files, so every language gets its own set, and each worker is handed one when the crew is built:
const w = defaultWorker(mult);
const names = localization.getRaw('game.warehouse.workerNames');
if (Array.isArray(names) && names.length) {
w.name = String(names[i % names.length]);
}There is one detail underneath this that I am oddly proud of. When the crew is first created, I do not start everyone at level one with zero progress. I spread them out — different starting levels, random progress inside each level:
w.level = 1 + (i % 4); // levels 1..4, staggered by index
w.xp = Math.floor(Math.random() * this._xpMax(w)); // random progress inside the level
w.points = (w.level - 1); // stat points earned for those levelsThe reason is human, not mechanical. If every worker starts identically, they all cross a level-up threshold at the same instant, and the little level-up sound fires for the whole crew at once — a jarring chord. Staggering their progress means level-ups arrive one at a time, like a room where someone occasionally has good news. A player will never know this code exists. They will only feel that the warehouse is alive rather than synchronized.
Stats borrowed wholesale
Each worker is a tiny object. Three stats starting at one, a level, experience, unspent points, and six equipment slots:
function defaultWorker(xpSpeedMult) {
return {
name: null,
stats: { str: 1, dex: 1, int: 1 },
level: 1,
xp: 0,
points: 0,
xpSpeedMult,
equipment: { head: -1, lh: -1, rh: -1, body: -1, ll: -1, rl: -1 },
};
}I did not decide what STR, DEX, and INT should mean from scratch — I mapped them onto the warehouse’s own economy in the most obvious way, and left it legible. The tuning lives in one small config block:
const WAREHOUSE_CONFIG = {
workerCount: 9,
speed: { base: 0.68, dexBonus: 0.05, dexDominantMult: 1.4 }, // DEX → work speed
xp: { baseRate: 1.5, intBonus: 0.06, maxLevel: 10 }, // INT → faster leveling
equipment: {
tierMult: [1.1, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0], // five tiers of gear
craftTime: [180, 360, 600, 1200, 2400], // higher tiers take longer to forge
},
bonus: { statWeight: 0.01, equipWeight: 0.02, focusMult: 2.0 },
};Dexterity makes a worker move and work faster. Intelligence makes them gain experience faster, so a smart worker levels up sooner and gets more stat points to spend. Strength (with the others) feeds the warehouse’s output bonus. When a worker levels up, they earn a point, and the player decides where it goes — the one genuine choice in the system, and a familiar one to anyone who has ever leveled a character.
Loot, and forging it through the factory
Workers wear equipment across six slots — head, two hands, body, two legs — and equipment comes in five tiers, each a stronger multiplier than the last. The inventory is a plain tally of what you own, keyed by slot and tier.
But here is the part I find most satisfying, because it is where two systems that had no reason to meet ended up interlocking. Equipment is not bought from a menu. It is manufactured on the production grid, by the same factory mechanic from Part 2.
There are forge buildings — Head Forge, Body Forge, Arms Forge, Legs Forge — that you place on the production grid like any other building. They consume the intermediate resources plasma and alloy (this, incidentally, is why those two resources matter: the forges are their reason to exist), and over a craft time they output a finished piece of equipment straight into the warehouse’s inventory:
_tickWarehouseForge(game, originKey, building, bt) {
// Needs a valid supply of its input resource, like any producer:
const supplyCheck = this._hasValidSupplier(originKey);
if (!supplyCheck.valid) { building._active = false; return false; }
// Spend a percentage of the connected input (plasma / alloy):
// …spend inputs…
// The forge's LEVEL decides the tier of gear it makes; higher tier = longer craft:
const tierIdx = Math.min(5, Math.max(1, building.level)) - 1;
const craftSec = WAREHOUSE_CONFIG.equipment.craftTime[tierIdx];
// …after craftSec of feeding, call game.warehouse.addCraftedItem(…)
}Upgrade the forge, and it produces higher-tier gear that takes proportionally longer to make. So the production factory — the core loop of the game — becomes the supply chain for the RPG bolted onto the side. You build stars into stardust into plasma, feed the plasma into a forge, and out comes a helmet for a worker named Bean, who is now slightly better at his job. Two systems the player thinks of as separate are, underneath, one continuous pipe.
The honest bottom of it
For all the RPG texture — names, levels, stats, six slots, five tiers of loot — I should show where it lands, because this series is about telling the truth under the surface. Everything the warehouse does resolves, in the end, to a single multiplier on your income. The whole crew, all their stats, all their gear, collapses into one number:
const baseMult = 1 + totalStats * b.statWeight + totalEquip * b.equipWeight;
const focus = resourceId === this.focusResource ? b.focusMult : 1;Add up every worker’s stats, add up every piece of equipment, weight them, and you get a bonus like “×4.8” applied to a chosen resource. That is the warehouse. Underneath the role-playing costume it is the same sentence as the rest of the game — a new amount equals a base plus its bonuses — the equation I opened this whole series with. The RPG does not change the math. It changes how the math feels: a multiplier you assembled out of named characters you leveled and equipped reads as yours in a way that the same multiplier bought from a tech node never does.
There is a small echo here I only noticed after I finished. Long before Pure Idle became a clicker, one of my abandoned prototypes was a top-down game about little units that lived and worked on their own while a player watched from above and occasionally intervened. That idea died as a game. Then it walked back in through the warehouse: a crew of workers who move, level, team up, and occasionally lose their cargo on their own, until the player steps in to redistribute a stat point or equip a better helmet. I did not do that on purpose. Apparently it is just the kind of thing I want to make, and it will find its way into whatever I build until I let it be the main thing.
The warehouse is the one place where I let the clicker stop being a clicker. Everything else in the game is the honest, bare loop. Here I let it dress up. The next part goes to the opposite extreme — the systems the player is never supposed to notice at all: offline income, the settings menu, switching languages, and wiring up Steam achievements. The invisible work that a game does not feel finished without.
